A||||54||jeffndebrus||3800 vs 4.9caddy||||1||
Z||000000||jeffndebrus||11-03-2001||09:54 AM||||this ought to get some things stirred up!<BR>I want a long term (reliable) solution to Fiero power.  I have an 85 se 2.8 auto at present.  I would like to redo the car a little at a time including a powerful new reliable and economic engine.  I am not looking for a five dollar engine swap here.  I want what will be best for years to come.  Please discuss these issues and also cost differences in these two popular engine swaps.||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||11-03-2001||12:30 PM||||If reliable power is your only consideration, I'd go with the 3800. I have see so many cars equipped with the 3800 rolling up over 200,000 miles. Solid engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jeffndebrus||11-03-2001||12:43 PM||||thank you,<BR>what other things should i consider?||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||11-03-2001||12:43 PM||||Second that on the 3800, couple of my friends have them, they're salemen, and put on a lot of miles. A few of them have more than 250,000 miles on them and the engines have never been apart.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jeffndebrus||11-03-2001||12:54 PM||||who is the best one to buy a kit from then??<BR>also who is best at doing 3800 swaps if i want them to do it for me??||||reg||1
Z||000005||ray b||11-03-2001||04:10 PM||||caddy 4.9 is lighter by a bunch 20% or 70 lbs<BR>and maybe cheaper to buy toooo esp the superchager 3800. plus is a V-8 and not a problem motor like early 4.1 was. less eazy power than SC pully swap gives hard to call. get local prices on both then see, may come down to best local deal on a good low mile motor 8 or 6.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd<p>[This message has been edited by ray b (edited 11-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||87FieroGTx||11-03-2001||05:27 PM|||| 3800 by a long shot!<P>------------------<BR>85 Fiero GT ~ auto 3.2 200hp 220tq ~ 75+ shot NOS ~ 0-60 4.9 13.5 1/4||||reg||1
Z||000007||MooCow||11-03-2001||05:56 PM||||Well if any of you know Taz's car from The Fiero Factory he has a 3800 and he has switched it out and installed a 4.9.  The reason was because the 4.9 caddy is far cheaper and according to Ed Parks damn close in reliablilty.  Also it fits in the engine bay a lot easier and if far cheaper to install.  If you have any question about the 4.9 just email Ed Parks at: fierofactory@juno.com<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forum_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||11-03-2001||09:55 PM||||Power output is about the same. The 3800 is a beter engine as far as design. The 4.9 has an aluminum block with iron heads and floating boreliners, not necesarily bad or unreliable, just strange. Believe it or not, the 4.9 is a better/easier fit, I've done both....whithout any kits. If I were loaded I'd go with a new supercharged 3800, they kick ass. On the other hand if I were me (not loaded) I'd go with the 4.9 because it's cheap. The only reason to choose a 4.9 over a 3800 is lower cost/ need a V8/ be different (at least for now).||||reg||1
Z||000009||jeffndebrus||11-03-2001||11:06 PM||||Thank you all-- it sounds like the 4.9 is gaining popularity quickly. It also sounds like it is a good fit and power option for the fiero. I guess I will go with the 4.9 swap---now to figure out the rest---maybe I should wait for the ed parks kit to become available. I sure don't want to get into something that I cannot finish or get to run. I am not a mechanic by trade and just want to enjoy my fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Daleford||11-03-2001||11:18 PM||||Just brought my 4.9 powered Fiero chassis home from my mechanic tonight. The most important reason to go with it over the 3800 is:   THE SOUND !!!<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000011||fieroX||11-04-2001||04:41 AM||||I put in a vote for the 3800.  first off look at its heritage.  the Grand national and GNX engines are old school versions of the series II supercharged.  I know at the moment there arent any series II's making near the hp of the GN's but give it some time.  These engines are the future.  Look at the up and coming aftermarket.  What kind of aftermarket does the caddy engine have?  I havent researched it so Im asking not flaming the engine.  I dont know alot about the caddy, but I do know alot about the L67.  I know that some grand national engines are making 800+ hp on the stock block and crank.  Just give the 3800 some time 10 years from now we are going to be saying, man back in 2001 these engines only made 240 horse stock.  Look at them now.<BR>Oh and about sound as mentioned in the post before this one, there is nothing I love better than a supercharger screaming out "get the hell out of my way!"<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||jeffndebrus||11-04-2001||07:22 AM||||i THINK bUBBAJOE DESCRIBED THE SOUND OF THE SUPERCHARGER KICKING IN AS AN INJURED DUCK.<BR>iF IT SOUNDS ANYTHING LIKE THESE HONDAS AND TOYOTAS RUNNING AROUND HERE AT 11,000 RPMS WITH THEIR FART CANS ON THE BACK i DON'T THINK i COULD STAND TO DRIVE IT. lmao||||reg||1
Z||000013||rigdonhome||11-04-2001||08:06 AM||||I'm also interested in the 3800/4.9 possibilities...  I know that the 3800 and supercharged 3800 are both probably multi-port fuel injection, and probably more efficient engines...  a little more high-tech, too.  <P>I don't know this about the 4.9...  Is it throttle body injected only?  What years was it made?  Is it still made?  <P>I like to stay at least at the same level of technology when I do a swap...  The stock 2.8L V6 is multi-port fuel injection, so I wouldn't really want to go backwards in technology...  Just my own opinion.<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000014||rockcrawl||11-04-2001||08:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>...there is nothing I love better than a supercharger screaming out "get the hell out of my way!"<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The supercharger on a 3800 doesn't scream anything....it silent. I don't think granny would appreciate blower whine on her Park Avenue! The only noise it makes is the rattling of the gears in the nose drive when it's worn out at 80,000 miles.<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I wouldn't really want to go backwards in technology...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> I agree totally. All '90 4.5s and all 4.9s have sequential port fuel injection.<P>Dale, you are right about the sound. You can make a v6 loud, but you can't make it sound like a V8. Even a quiet V8 sounds powerful. Anyone who's been around cars and hotrodding knows the difference. ||||reg||7
Z||000015||westtexas||11-04-2001||08:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>The only noise it makes is the rattling of the gears in the nose drive when it's worn out at 80,000 miles.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is it hard and/or expensive to repair the nose drive? <P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||jeffndebrus||11-04-2001||09:57 AM||||when i go to the U-Pull-A-Part are they going to nickle and dime me to death or can I just tell them that I need a 4.9 with its transmission and harness etc??<BR>Wonder if they will charge me for the fuel inj, starter, alternator, ecm, etc???<BR>some have said it is cheaper to buy a whole car but they want 6,000 gran for a 91 cadillac??  Wonder where is the best place to get what we need?||||reg||1
Z||000017||Daleford||11-04-2001||10:17 AM||||I wouldn't leave to the U-Pick with the feeling that you have all you need. You may be making several trips back. This is a complicated task and you need a lot of gizmos from the Cadillac plus a couple of whatzits.<BR>Look for an insurance write-off that still runs. This way you can check out a running engine before you buy, and when you drag it home, you know you have everything. Possibly someone will buy the left over Cadillac bodyparts. [I have some for sale, by the way]<BR>I was lucky. I bought a damaged 93 Deville from a bodyshop that had already used the parts they needed. My cost? $500.! And I was almost able to drive it home!<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000018||jeffndebrus||11-04-2001||12:40 PM||||bumpity bump bump bumpity bump||||reg||1
Z||000019||rigdonhome||11-04-2001||02:15 PM||||The 4.9 is sounding sweeter all the time...  <P>What years and models of the Caddy's have the multi-port sequential fuel injection?<P>Are there ANY hop-up parts for the 4.9?<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000020||rockcrawl||11-04-2001||02:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Is it hard and/or expensive to repair the nose drive?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Hard? No. Expensive? Yes. Bearings are not available separately, you need to buy a complete assembly for several hundred dollars.<P>jeffndebrus, I hope I didn't talk you out of a 3800. The 3800SC is a better engine if you can afford it. It's also an easier swap in that it's been done so many times and anyone can tell you how to do it. There are still a few things about the 4.9 that are up in the air. I suggest you keep gathering info for a while before going out and buying an engine.<BR> If you want a good deal you just have to wait and keep looking. I get 4.9s at the U-Pull-It for $100 including harness, ECM, all accesories, everything. That's the way to go if you know what you need. I've bought four of them that way (Thanks Eric_PA, V8Dreamer and foxgapfieros for helping me pull them, I owe you guys). If you don't get them quick, more parts will be missing. The ISC motor and ECM are the first to go, but I don't use them anyway. If you go to a full service yard you won't have to do any work, and you will get a complete engine for $400-$600, but you won't get the ECM or harness with it. I also bought a '91 DeVille for $350, and I was able to test drive it. I guess it depends where you live and how long you want to wait. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||jeffndebrus||11-04-2001||06:21 PM||||Thank you so much! All of you for your thoughts and input.<BR>Rockcrawl, I love your site as well.<BR>I guess I will be trying to get my 2.8 on the road for now and do a wait and see on the rest. I still have a new crated 4.0 aurora engine available for $400 and would love to put it in my 85 se but---- noone seems to know much about it.||||reg||1
Z||000022||fieroX||11-04-2001||11:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR>[B] [QUOTE]...there is nothing I love better than a supercharger screaming out "get the hell out of my way!"<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The supercharger on a 3800 doesn't scream anything....it silent. I don't think granny would appreciate blower whine on her Park Avenue! The only noise it makes is the rattling of the gears in the nose drive when it's worn out at 80,000 miles.<BR>[QUOTE]<P>have you heard one without an airbox, and with a ported blower output running a 3.0 pulley at 15lbs of boost?  Thats screaming.||||reg||1
Z||000023||MinnGreenGT||11-05-2001||10:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B> You can make a v6 loud, but you can't make it sound like a V8. Even a quiet V8 sounds powerful. Anyone who's been around cars and hotrodding knows the difference. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not exactly sure about that... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  You'd have to hear mine to know for sure.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013191.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013191.html</A> <P>I'd agree that the 3800 or even the 3800S/C is the probably the best long-term motor solution.  I'm still gearing up to try a 4.9L Caddy though- just really sounds like fun!<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||jeffndebrus||11-06-2001||11:01 PM||||anyone know what it will cost to have Ed Parks put a newly rebuilt 4.9 in??<P>would love to know!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000025||DrDave||11-06-2001||11:08 PM||||   He's still in the final stages of development, but tetitive pricing shows Auto kits at $2000, and Manual Kits adding $1200 to that.   He's trying to make the kit professional quality.  If I lived closer, I'd save my money and have him do it.  It would be CLEAN!   But....guess I'll just have to get my hands dirty.||||reg||1
Z||000026||banditbalz||11-06-2001||11:16 PM||||I would go for the 4.9 It makes for a lighter setup (aluminum heads and block), creates more hp (300 vs 240), comes equipped with DOHC and is far more reliable. First major scheduled tuneup in a 4.9 is 100,000km. Iv'e heard of problems with the Gen1 3800s/c so stay away from these. The Northstar just sounds better (I have heard both in fieros at full throttle) and it looks better in the engine  bay (read: fills the bay).<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/blackwhite2_3.jpg">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by banditbalz (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||Slammed Fiero||11-07-2001||12:35 AM||||Must be a late night for banditbalz!! <P>the 4.9 is not he N*. The Northstar is a 4.6.<BR>The engine their talking about here is the predecessor to the Northstar.<P>It's Iron head aluminum block , weighs the same as a 2.8. I have heard from several people that it's quite an easy swap. Check your local supershopper and you will see 4.9's for less than $500.<P>The northstar is a light motor ..the Autotranny that the N* uses is not <P>I would go with the 4.9 personally..dare to be different. You can put some NOS on it and if it blows up , an entire repacement engine is cheap.<P>John moss , GM monkier  / hot rodder put one in his 96 Monte Carlo in place of the 3.8. He just wanted that V8 Sound. <P>The performance attributes of the motor reminds me of an L98 350. Pulls like a bull dozer at low Rpm , great autocross motor. Pukes at about 4500rpm and afterwards is just making noise.<P>Great motor to build a cheap sleeper. pick up a auto tranny coupe (not GT ,,Sleeper!) Stuff one of those V8's in and have some fun with the locals!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000028||fieroX||11-07-2001||04:35 AM||||sounds like a pretty good plug for the caddy to me.  I dont know what kind of power the 4.9 makes, but Id like to line up next to one some time when my L67 is finished, well mostly because its going to see this....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR> <IMG SRC="http://home.kscable.com/ccheney/ryanpict/fiero10.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||Wannabe||11-07-2001||09:46 AM||||The Caddy swap itself sounds attractive, but an engine that gives up at 4500rpm doesn't sound like much fun for a small "sportster" like the Fiero. Also, I have read that the Caddy torque comes on so soon that it's hard to get it to the ground. Can't argue with the attraction of a V8 though. On the other hand, the 3800 is also know for a good wide torque curve, and with some minor mods, will pull to 6000 rpm. The other thing to keep in mind is that there is a growing aftermarket for the 3800. Now if only someone would come out with  aluminum heads for the 3800, it might lose that extra 50 or so pounds it adds.||||reg||1
Z||000030||lou_dias||11-07-2001||12:17 PM||||Come out with aluminum heads for the 3800?<P>Aren't all series II and above aluminum?<P>it's the block that's cast iron.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Wannabe||11-07-2001||12:37 PM||||Unfortunately not. All iron, but supposedly that is part of the reason for the 3.8's good reliability record. Main drawback is that is limits the amount of boost the engine can handle without knock as compared to Al heads.||||reg||1
Z||000032||Ferrari_cdn||11-07-2001||03:34 PM||||What are the 1/4 times and 0-60 times for both the Caddy 4.9 and the 3800SC?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">  </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000033||lou_dias||11-07-2001||05:38 PM||||Doesn't the SC3400 prototyped in the new grand am make more hp than the 3800SC?<BR>I bet in time that will be the most popular swap since the aluminum heads on that will make for a lighter package than the 3800...and more hp to boot.||||reg||1
Z||000034||Mickey_Moose||11-07-2001||06:31 PM||||Was there not a web page someplace that had all the info on doing the 4.9 swap - thinking of this myself for the future, and was wondering what is all involved  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>thanks<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000035||Wannabe||11-07-2001||06:56 PM||||Prototypes always make more hp than the final production models. I believe the 3.8SC GP GTX put out around 300hp. Other protoypes included a 238hp na 3.4 and a 290hp na 3800. All these engines have plenty of potential. The key is if the parts needed to realize it are available.||||reg||1
